<NotePage slug="designing-for-every-player">
  <p class="lede">The same puzzle can feel relaxing to one person and stressful to another. Usually the difference isn’t the puzzle at all: it’s the ticking clock, the small text, or a red square that some people can’t tell apart from a green one. So we made those parts adjustable, and left the puzzles themselves alone.</p>

  <h2>The puzzle never changes</h2>
  <p>The first rule was that settings must not split players into different games. A daily Island Sketch is the same grid whichever style you choose, and a star means the same thing for everyone: three stars, minus one per hint. What changes is everything <em>around</em> the board.</p>

  <h2>Three play styles</h2>
  <p>The first time you visit, Pip asks how you like to play. You can change your answer any time in <a href="/settings">Settings</a>.</p>

  <h3>Sprout: no clock, a helping hand</h3>
  <p>Sprout hides the timer and personal-best times completely. A timer is meant to be harmless, but for many people it quietly turns a calm puzzle into a race. Sprout also makes the toolbar buttons bigger and easier to tap.</p>
  <p>If you pause for about thirty seconds, Pip gently offers to point at a good spot. The wording matters here. The message starts “Taking a little think? That’s great,” because pausing to think is the whole point of a logic puzzle, not a sign that something has gone wrong.</p>

  <h3>Explorer: the classic</h3>
  <p>Explorer is the default: a quiet timer, and hints whenever you want them. Nothing nudges you.</p>

  <h3>Master: personal bests and flawless runs</h3>
  <p>Master is for players who like a challenge on top of the puzzle. Alongside your personal bests, it marks a solve with no hints as flawless. Because a single stray tap on Hint would spoil that, the first hint in a puzzle asks you to confirm.</p>

  <h2>Text that fits your eyes</h2>
  <p>Settings offers three text sizes. The largest raises body text from 16 to 20 pixels across guides, rules and menus.</p>

  <h2>Clearer patterns: never colour alone</h2>
  <p>Roughly one man in twelve and one woman in two hundred have some form of colour-vision deficiency. Red and green, the classic “wrong” and “right” colours, are the pair most often confused.</p>
  <p>Turning on <em>Clearer patterns</em> adds a second signal that doesn’t depend on colour:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>In Isle Spans, a finished island gets a thicker outline, an island with too many bridges gets a dashed one, and a wrong bridge is drawn dashed.</li>
    <li>In Lighthouse Wiring, wires that aren’t lit yet are drawn dotted, so you can see which part of the network is connected without judging shades.</li>
    <li>Wrong tiles get a dashed border as well as a red tint.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Even with the setting off, we try to make sure the important states show up as shape as well as colour: crosses for squares you’ve ruled out in Island Sketch, and numbers on every island in Isle Spans.</p>

  <h2>Calm motion</h2>
  <p>Celebrations, sparkles and page transitions make a solve feel good, but motion can be uncomfortable for some people. If your device is set to reduce motion, the site respects that: animations are toned down or skipped, and scrolling jumps instead of gliding.</p>

  <h2>Your progress is yours</h2>
  <p>There are no accounts. Settings, stars, streaks and stickers are saved in your own browser and never sent to us. The <a href="/progress">Journal</a> shows everything that’s been saved and lets you reset it. The trade-off is that progress doesn’t follow you to another device, but nobody has to hand over an email address to do a puzzle.</p>

  <h2>Tell us what’s missing</h2>
  <p>If something on the site is hard for you to see, read or tap, please <a href="/contact">tell us</a>. Changes like these help everyone, not only the people who asked for them.</p>
</NotePage>
